      "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The coronavirus chewed through Sysco\u2019s core grub-supplying business as restaurants around the world shuttered last year. Revenues for Sysco, America\u2019s biggest wholesale food distributor, fell 12% year on year to $52.9 billion, a decline that caused it to slide four places in the Fortune 500 rankings. COVID-19 took a bite out of profits, too, causing a stomach-churning 87% drop to $215 million. The Houston-based company pivoted amid pandemic-related closures to help its eatery customers offer alternative options, like takeout-meal kits and contactless menus. But Sysco\u2019s financial prospects are mostly tied to people\u2019s desire to dine out again as the economy reopens.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri, Arial&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\" data-sheets-textstyleruns=\"{&quot;1&quot;:0}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:280,&quot;2&quot;:{&quot;6&quot;:1}}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:287}\">The coronavirus chewed through Sysco\u2019s core grub-supplying business as restaurants around the world shuttered last year. Revenues for Sysco, America\u2019s biggest wholesale food distributor, fell 12% year on year to $52.9 billion, a decline that caused it to slide four places in the Fortune 500 rankings. COVID-19 took a bite out of profits, too, causing a stomach-churning 87% drop to $215 million. The Houston-based company pivoted amid pandemic-related closures to help its eatery customers offer alternative options, like takeout-meal kits and contactless menus. But Sysco\u2019s financial prospects are mostly tied to people\u2019s desire to dine out again as the economy reopens.</span>",
